window.SUBJECTS.push({"id":"chem","n":"Chemistry","tab":"Chemistry","w":"Matter and solutions, reactions, and chemistry in daily life.","topics":[{"id":"matter","n":"Matter, Atoms and Solutions","qs":[{"id":"q0276","q":"Which of the following is an example of a colloidal solution commonly found in the kitchen?","o":["Salt dissolved in water","Coffee","Oil and vinegar in salad dressing","Sugar dissolved in water"],"a":1,"x":"DSSSB's key takes brewed coffee as the colloidal solution. Salt or sugar in water is a true solution.","e":"Other kitchen colloids: milk, butter, jelly, mayonnaise.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S2"],"k":"An oil-and-vinegar dressing is an emulsion, which is also a kind of colloid; the key still marks coffee."},{"id":"q0731","q":"Sugar has the ability to absorb moisture from the surrounding it is exposed to. One of the following terms that describes this is known as ___________.","o":["Eroding","Condensation","Activation","Hygroscopy"],"a":3,"x":"Hygroscopy is a substance's ability to absorb moisture from the air; sugar and common salt are hygroscopic.","e":"Deliquescent substances absorb so much water that they dissolve, such as calcium chloride.","src":["Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 31 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1748","q":"Which of the following statement is correct? I. Molecular mass of substance is sum of atomic mass of all the atoms in a molecule. II. Number of moles = molar mass/given mass III. Molar mass is number of moles present in 1 gram of substance.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"x":"Molecular mass is the sum of the atomic masses of all atoms in a molecule. Moles = given mass ÷ molar mass (not the reverse), and molar mass is the mass of one mole, so only statement I is right.","e":"Moles = mass ÷ molar mass.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1791","q":"Which of the following statement regarding modern periodic table is correct? I. Atomic radius increases in moving left to right along the period. II. Atomic size decreases down the group.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":3,"x":"In the modern periodic table atomic radius DECREASES from left to right (more nuclear charge) and INCREASES down a group (more shells), so both statements are wrong.","e":"Across a period: smaller; down a group: bigger.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1834","q":"Which of the following was proposed by J.J. Thompson? I. Atom consists of a positively charges sphere. II. Electrons revolve around the positively charged sphere. III. The negative and positive charges are equal in magnitude.","o":["I and II","II and III","I and III","I, II and III"],"a":2,"x":"J.J. Thomson's 'plum pudding' model: the atom is a positively charged sphere with electrons embedded in it, and positive and negative charges are equal. Electrons revolving around a nucleus came later with Rutherford and Bohr.","e":"Thomson = plum pudding; Rutherford = nucleus; Bohr = orbits.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1985","q":"Which of the following statement is/are correct? I. Isotope of uranium is used in treatment of goiter. II. Isotope of cobalt is used in treatment of cancer.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":1,"x":"Radioactive iodine (I-131), not uranium, treats goitre and thyroid disorders; cobalt-60 is used in cancer radiotherapy. So only II is correct.","e":"Iodine → thyroid; cobalt → cancer; carbon-14 → dating.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2031","q":"Which of the following pair is/are correct? I. Isotopes – different mass numbers II. Isobars – different atomic numbers","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Isotopes have the same atomic number but different mass numbers; isobars have the same mass number but different atomic numbers. Both pairs are correct.","e":"Isotopes: same Z; isobars: same A.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2073","q":"Which of the following relation is correct? I. Atomic number = Number of protons + Number of electrons II. Mass number = Number of protons + Number of neutrons","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":1,"x":"Atomic number = number of protons only (not protons + electrons); mass number = protons + neutrons. So only II is correct.","e":"Z = protons; A = protons + neutrons.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2112","q":"Which of the following statement regarding metals and non-metals is NOT correct? I. Silver is the most ductile metal. II. Gallium and Caesium have high melting points. III. Alkali metals have high densitics.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"All three are wrong: gold (not silver) is the most ductile metal; gallium and caesium have very low melting points (they melt in the hand); alkali metals have low densities.","e":"Gallium melts at about 30°C.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2440","q":"Which of the following elements has 2 electrons in K-shell and 2 electrons in L-shell?","o":["Lithium","Beryllium","Boron","Carbon"],"a":1,"x":"Beryllium (atomic number 4) has the electronic configuration 2, 2: two electrons in the K shell and two in the L shell.","e":"Li 2,1; Be 2,2; B 2,3; C 2,4.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2443","q":"Due to which of the following phenomenon is a plasma created?","o":["High energization of gases","Super-cooling of solids","Ionization of liquids","Super-cooling of liquids"],"a":0,"x":"Plasma, the fourth state of matter, forms when a gas is given so much energy that its atoms are ionised; stars and neon signs contain plasma.","e":"Solid, liquid, gas, plasma (and Bose–Einstein condensate).","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2526","q":"What is the colour of pure acetic acid?","o":["White","Yellow","Green","Colourless"],"a":3,"x":"Pure acetic acid is a colourless liquid; below about 17°C it freezes into ice-like crystals, hence the name glacial acetic acid.","e":"Vinegar is dilute acetic acid.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2597","q":"Modern periodic table has ______ groups and ______ periods.","o":["16; 7","18; 7","18; 5","16; 5"],"a":1,"x":"The modern periodic table has 18 vertical groups and 7 horizontal periods.","e":"Groups = columns; periods = rows.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2908","q":"Which of the following statement regarding metals is correct? I. Property of metals by which they can be beaten into thin sheets is called ductility. II. Property of metals by which they can be drawn into wires is called malleability.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":3,"x":"Both definitions are swapped: being beaten into thin sheets is malleability, being drawn into wires is ductility.","e":"Gold is the most malleable and ductile metal.","src":["Junior Clerk 17 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2909","q":"Which of the following relation of number of atoms is correct?","o":["Number of atoms = (given mass/molar mass) × avogadro number","Number of atoms = (given mass/avogadro number) × molar mass","Number of atoms × avogadro number = given mass × molar mass","Avogadro number/number of atoms = molar mass × given mass"],"a":0,"x":"Number of atoms (or molecules) = (given mass ÷ molar mass) × Avogadro's number (6.022 × 10²³).","e":"Moles × NA = number of particles.","src":["Junior Clerk 17 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1301","q":"Which of the following elements has zero valency?","o":["Lithium","Beryllium","Helium","Fluorine"],"a":2,"x":"Helium, a noble gas, has a full outer shell (duet) and does not normally form bonds, so its valency is zero.","e":"Other noble gases: neon, argon, krypton, xenon, radon.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4892","q":"What is the chemical formula of Methane?","o":["CH4","C2H4","C4H4","C8H18"],"a":0,"x":"Methane is CH₄, the simplest hydrocarbon and the main part of natural gas.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 26 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4893","q":"The elements that have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons are called?","o":["Raibotopes","Isotopes","Galavnotopes","Isomers"],"a":1,"x":"Isotopes are atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons.","e":"Isobars: same mass number, different elements.","src":["Stenographer 26 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5020","q":"Who among the following contributed more to the ’quantum model of Hydrogen atom’?","o":["Enrico Fermi","CH Townes","Hideki Yukawa","Niels Bohr"],"a":3,"x":"Niels Bohr gave the quantum model of the hydrogen atom (1913), with electrons in fixed energy orbits.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 26 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5021","q":"Which of the following elements have atomic weight 23 and 35.5, respectively?","o":["P and S","Mg and Al","K and Ca","Na and Cl"],"a":3,"x":"Sodium has atomic mass 23 and chlorine 35.5.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 26 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6953","q":"Which of the following elements does not belong to the Halogen group of Periodic Table?","o":["Fluorine","Iodine","Bromine","Helium"],"a":3,"x":"Helium is a noble gas; fluorine, bromine and iodine are halogens (group 17).","e":"","src":["Stenographer 8 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q5405","q":"Which of the following is a negatively charged particle in an atom?","o":["Electron","Proton","Neutron","Positron"],"a":0,"x":"The electron carries a negative charge, the proton a positive charge and the neutron none.","e":"","src":["Personal Assistant 29 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4486","q":"Which of the following elements is found in Stainless Steel that gives the property of resistance to corrosion?","o":["Chromium","Helium","Carbon","Neon"],"a":0,"x":"Chromium in stainless steel forms a protective oxide layer that resists corrosion.","e":"","src":["Office Superintendent 24 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q9005","q":"A fatty acid contains which functional group?","o":["Amino group","Carboxyl group","Phosphate group","Hydroxyl group"],"a":1,"x":"Fatty acids contain a carboxyl group (–COOH).","e":"","src":["MTS 3 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q9226","q":"Which element is a key component of organic molecules, such as carbohydrates, lipids, proteins, and nucleic acids?","o":["Oxygen","Nitrogen","Carbon","Hydrogen"],"a":2,"x":"Carbon is the key element in all organic molecules: carbohydrates, lipids, proteins and nucleic acids.","e":"","src":["MTS 8 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q3422","q":"Which of the following pair is correct? I. Argon – Monoatomic II. Hydrogen – Diatomic","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Argon, a noble gas, exists as single atoms (monoatomic); hydrogen exists as H₂ molecules (diatomic).","e":"","src":["Store Keeper 19 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3675","q":"How many maximum number of naturally occuring elements are there on the earth?","o":["87","90","94","102"],"a":2,"x":"Commonly given as 94 naturally occurring elements, up to plutonium, which occurs in traces.","e":"","src":["Fire Operator 20 Apr 2021 S3"],"k":"NCERT Class 9 says about 92 elements occur naturally; DSSSB's key uses the figure 94, which counts trace natural technetium, promethium, neptunium and plutonium."},{"id":"q3211","q":"Which of the following is a p-block element?","o":["Boron","Lithium","Sodium","Francium"],"a":0,"x":"Boron (group 13) is a p-block element; lithium, sodium and francium are s-block alkali metals.","e":"p-block: groups 13–18.","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 18 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3212","q":"What is the molecular mass of Butane?","o":["44","52","58","62"],"a":2,"x":"Butane (C₄H₁₀) has molecular mass 4 × 12 + 10 × 1 = 58.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 18 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3254","q":"Which of the following is an s-block element?","o":["Caesium","Boron","Nitrogen","Argon"],"a":0,"x":"Caesium (group 1) is an s-block element; boron, nitrogen and argon are p-block.","e":"s-block: groups 1 and 2.","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 18 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3255","q":"What is the molecular-mass of Pentane?","o":["66","72","78","86"],"a":1,"x":"Pentane (C₅H₁₂) has molecular mass 5 × 12 + 12 × 1 = 72.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 18 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q6353","q":"Which of the following is a property of a metal? I. Lustrous II. Good conductor of heat III. Hard","o":["I and II","II and III","I and III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"Metals are generally lustrous, hard and good conductors of heat and electricity (sodium and potassium are soft exceptions).","e":"","src":["Care Taker 8 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q5487","q":"Which of the following gases is found in the highest amount in the atmosphere?","o":["Nitrogen","Oxygen","Argon","Carbon dioxide"],"a":0,"x":"Nitrogen (about 78%) is the most abundant gas in the atmosphere.","e":"","src":["Accountant 29 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0908","q":"Which of the following is NOT a colligative property?","o":["Freezing point depression","Relative lowering of vapour pressure","Elevation of freezing point","Boiling point elevation"],"a":2,"x":"Colligative properties depend only on the number of solute particles: lowering of vapour pressure, elevation of boiling point, depression of freezing point and osmotic pressure. The freezing point falls, not rises.","e":"Salt on icy roads works by depressing the freezing point.","src":["PRT 17 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q0935","q":"Which classification method was proposed by J.A.R. Newlands?","o":["Group theory","Periodic table","Law of triads","Law of octaves"],"a":3,"x":"John Newlands (1865) proposed the Law of Octaves: when elements are arranged by atomic mass, every eighth element resembles the first, like musical notes.","e":"Döbereiner's triads (1817) came earlier; Mendeleev's periodic table (1869) later.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q0951","q":"Elements with atomic numbers 90 to 103 are classified as _______.","o":["Lanthanoids","Alkali metals","Transition elements","Actinoids"],"a":3,"x":"Elements with atomic numbers 90 (thorium) to 103 (lawrencium) are the actinoids; 58–71 are the lanthanoids. Both are f-block elements.","e":"Lanthanoids 58–71, actinoids 90–103.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1077","q":"The modern Periodic Law is based on ________.","o":["Rutherford Scattering Law","Bohr’s Atomic Law","Octave Law","Moseley's Law"],"a":3,"x":"The modern periodic law (Moseley, 1913): properties of elements are a periodic function of their atomic numbers, not atomic masses.","e":"Mendeleev's law was based on atomic mass; Newlands proposed the law of octaves.","src":["PRT 23 Feb 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1144","q":"A homogeneous mixture of two or more components is a solution. The component present in the largest quantity is generally known as the:","o":["solute","vapour","solvent","precipitate"],"a":2,"x":"In a solution, the component present in the largest amount is the solvent; the smaller components are solutes.","e":"In salt water, water is the solvent and salt the solute.","src":["PRT 26 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1175","q":"180 g of glucose (C6H12O6) is dissolved in 1 kg of water. At what temperature will water boil at 1.013 bar (Kb for water is 0.52K kg mol-1)?","o":["373.67 K","373.15 K","373.27 K","373.202 K"],"a":0,"x":"180 g of glucose is 1 mole; in 1 kg of water that is 1 molal. ΔTb = Kb × m = 0.52 × 1 = 0.52 K, so the boiling point = 373.15 + 0.52 = 373.67 K.","e":"Boiling-point elevation is a colligative property: it depends on the number of solute particles.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1188","q":"The concentration of a solution cannot be described by ___________.","o":["parts per million","volume by mass percentage (V/w)","mole fraction","mass by volume percentage (w/V)"],"a":1,"x":"Standard concentration terms include mass %, volume %, mass by volume % (w/V), ppm, mole fraction, molarity and molality. 'Volume by mass %' (V/w) is not used.","e":"ppm is used for very dilute solutions, such as fluoride in water.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1250","q":"In the context of solutions, which of the following is NOT a colligative property?","o":["Depression of freezing point of the solvent","Viscosity of the solution","Elevation of boiling point of the solvent","Osmotic pressure of the solution"],"a":1,"x":"Colligative properties depend on the number of solute particles: lowering of vapour pressure, depression of freezing point, elevation of boiling point and osmotic pressure. Viscosity is not one.","e":"Four colligatives: VP down, FP down, BP up, osmotic pressure.","src":["PRT 3 Apr 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q7837","q":"One molecule of citric acid contains how many hydrogen atoms?","o":["Two","Four","Six","Eight"],"a":3,"x":"Citric acid (C₆H₈O₇) has eight hydrogen atoms in a molecule.","e":"","src":["Nursery Teacher 24 Jun 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q7912","q":"Which of the following are insoluble in water?","o":["Carbohydrates","Sugars","Fats","Salts"],"a":2,"x":"Fats are insoluble in water; sugars, most carbohydrates and salts dissolve.","e":"","src":["Nursery Teacher 24 Jun 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q1519","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct about halogens?","o":["This is the only group that contains elements in all three states of matter at room temperature and pressure.","Halogens are group 17th elements.","Chlorine is the most electronegative element.","This is a group of highly reactive non-metals."],"a":2,"x":"The incorrect statement is that chlorine is the most electronegative element. Fluorine is. The others hold: halogens are group 17, highly reactive non-metals, and occur as gas (F₂, Cl₂), liquid (Br₂) and solid (I₂).","e":"Chlorine has the highest electron affinity; fluorine the highest electronegativity.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1537","q":"Which law states that the partial pressure of a gas in the vapour phase is proportional to the mole fraction of the gas in the solution?","o":["Boyle's law","Charles's law","Henry's law","Raoult's law"],"a":2,"x":"Henry's law: the partial pressure of a gas in the vapour phase is proportional to its mole fraction in the solution (p = K_H·x) — gas solubility rises with pressure.","e":"That is why soft drinks are bottled under high CO₂ pressure.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q1654","q":"The atomic number of an element is the same as the number of ______.","o":["neutrons","protons","neutrons + protons","electrons + neutrons"],"a":1,"x":"An element's atomic number equals the number of protons in its nucleus.","e":"","src":["TGT 10 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1711","q":"Which among the following is the symbol of Lead?","o":["Al","Au","Pb","Tn"],"a":2,"x":"Pb (plumbum) is the symbol of lead.","e":"","src":["TGT 10 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1732","q":"Which among the following is the symbol of Silver?","o":["Ag","Hg","Pb","Sn"],"a":0,"x":"Ag (argentum) is the symbol of silver; Hg is mercury.","e":"","src":["TGT 10 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2291","q":"Who among the following has discovered Radium?","o":["Henry Becquerel","Michael Faraday","Enrico Ferni","Marie and Pierre Curie"],"a":3,"x":"Marie and Pierre Curie discovered radium (and polonium) in 1898.","e":"","src":["TGT 13 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2326","q":"What is the atomic number of cobalt?","o":["30","29","28","27"],"a":3,"x":"Cobalt (Co) has atomic number 27.","e":"","src":["TGT 14 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2328","q":"Which of the following statement is correct? I. Rate of evaporation increases with a increase in humidity. II. Evaporation is a surface phenomenon.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":1,"x":"Evaporation is a surface phenomenon, and it slows down, not speeds up, when humidity rises.","e":"","src":["TGT 14 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2348","q":"Which of the following is NOT a metalloid?","o":["Boron","Selenium","Germanium","Polonium"],"a":1,"x":"Selenium is a non-metal; boron, germanium and polonium are classed as metalloids.","e":"","src":["TGT 14 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2368","q":"Which of the following is an example of emulsion?","o":["Fog","Foam","Milk","Milk of magnesia"],"a":2,"x":"Milk is an emulsion (liquid fat dispersed in water); fog and foam are aerosol and gas-in-liquid colloids.","e":"","src":["TGT 14 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2369","q":"Which of the following metal is NOT found in nature in the free state?","o":["Gold","Silver","Aluminium","Copper"],"a":2,"x":"Aluminium is too reactive to occur free in nature; gold, silver and copper can occur in the free state.","e":"","src":["TGT 14 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3520","q":"What is the atomic number of chlorine?","o":["17","19","15","13"],"a":0,"x":"Chlorine has atomic number 17.","e":"","src":["TGT 1 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3569","q":"Which of the following is NOT an example of Alkali metal?","o":["Lithium","Magnesium","Sodium","Potassium"],"a":1,"x":"Magnesium is an alkaline earth metal; lithium, sodium and potassium are alkali metals.","e":"","src":["TGT 1 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4793","q":"Monazite sand does not contain _______.","o":["Thorium","lanthanum","Cerium","Actinium"],"a":3,"x":"Monazite sand contains thorium, cerium and lanthanum, but not actinium.","e":"","src":["TGT 25 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5040","q":"What is the molecular weight of heavy water?","o":["15","26","20","30"],"a":2,"x":"Heavy water (D₂O) has a molecular mass of about 20.","e":"","src":["TGT 26 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5861","q":"By which method the mixture of salt and camphor can be separated?","o":["Distillation","Sublimation","Chromatography","Fractional distillation"],"a":1,"x":"Camphor sublimes, so a mixture of salt and camphor can be separated by sublimation.","e":"","src":["TGT 6 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6046","q":"The chemical symbol of Gold is:","o":["Ag","Au","Al","Ga"],"a":1,"x":"Au (aurum) is the chemical symbol of gold.","e":"","src":["TGT 8 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6259","q":"Which pair of Non-Metal – Atomicity is correct? I. Nitrogen – Diatomic II. Phosphorus – Tetra -atomic","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Nitrogen (N₂) is diatomic and phosphorus (P₄) is tetra-atomic, so both pairs are correct.","e":"","src":["TGT 9 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q7531","q":"Which method of separation is used for seperating heavier and lighter components by bowing air?","o":["Threshing","Winnowing","Sieving","Hand picking"],"a":1,"x":"Winnowing separates heavier and lighter parts (grain and husk) using wind.","e":"","src":["TGT 7 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7575","q":"Property of metal by which it can be drawn into wires is called ______.","o":["malleability","ductility","sonority","lustrity"],"a":1,"x":"Ductility is the property by which metals can be drawn into wires; malleability is beating into sheets.","e":"","src":["TGT 8 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7966","q":"Following postulates about the model of an atom are given by which model of an atom? (i) Only certain special orbits known as discrete orbits of electrons, are allowed inside the atom. (ii) While revolving in discrete orbits the electrons do not radiate energy.","o":["BOHR’S MODEL OF ATOM","RUTHERFORD’S MODEL OF AN ATOM","THOMSON’S MODEL OF AN ATOM","DALTON’S MODEL OF AN ATOM"],"a":0,"x":"Bohr's model says electrons move only in certain discrete orbits and do not radiate energy while in them.","e":"","src":["TGT 6 Jul 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q7967","q":"According to which schemes/principles the outermost shell of an atom can accommodate a maximum of 8 electrons?","o":["Bohr-Bury scheme","Aufbau principle,","Pauli’s Exclusion principle","Hund’s rule."],"a":0,"x":"The Bohr–Bury scheme says the outermost shell of an atom can hold at most 8 electrons.","e":"","src":["TGT 6 Jul 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q8853","q":"What is formed when atoms of different elements combine in a definite ratio?","o":["A compound","A mixture","An element","An alloy"],"a":0,"x":"A compound forms when atoms of different elements combine in a fixed ratio.","e":"","src":["Special Educator 14 Mar 2026"]},{"id":"q0869","q":"Which law states that equal volumes of all gases at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules?","o":["Avogadro's law","Law of multiple proportions","Gay-Lussac's law of gaseous volumes","Law of conservation of mass"],"a":0,"x":"Avogadro's law: equal volumes of all gases at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules.","e":"1 mole of any gas at STP occupies 22.4 litres.","src":["PGT 24 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q6388","q":"___________ law states that the rates of diffusion of gases are inversely proportional to the square root of their densities under similar conditions of temperature and pressure.","o":["Charles'","Graham's","Pascal's","Stefan's"],"a":1,"x":"Graham's law: rates of diffusion of gases are inversely proportional to the square roots of their densities.","e":"","src":["PGT 18 Jul 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6612","q":"Which among the following is also known as 'white vitriol'?","o":["Sulphuric Acid","Zinc Sulphate","Sodium Carbonate","Calcium Carbonate"],"a":1,"x":"Zinc sulphate is known as white vitriol.","e":"Blue vitriol – copper sulphate; green vitriol – iron sulphate.","src":["PGT 4 Jul 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q7130","q":"Which of the following elements has a valency of 2?","o":["Beryllium","Nitrogen","Carbon","Silicon"],"a":0,"x":"Beryllium has a valency of 2; nitrogen is 3, and carbon and silicon are 4.","e":"","src":["PGT 31 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7190","q":"____________ are atoms, molecules, or ions of similar size containing the same number of atoms and valence electrons.","o":["Isotopes","Isobars","Isosteres","Isotones"],"a":2,"x":"Isosteres are atoms, molecules or ions of similar size with the same number of atoms and valence electrons.","e":"","src":["PGT 16 Jul 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q7326","q":"Which of the following is the lightest element on Earth?","o":["Helium","Nitrogen","Oxygen","Hydrogen"],"a":3,"x":"Hydrogen is the lightest element.","e":"","src":["PGT 31 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7666","q":"Which of the following is the hardest naturally occurring substance on Earth?","o":["Gold","Iron","Diamond","Platinum"],"a":2,"x":"Diamond is the hardest naturally occurring substance.","e":"","src":["PGT 29 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7689","q":"Which of the following is a malleable metal?","o":["Zinc","Sulphur","Phosphurus","Oxygen"],"a":0,"x":"Zinc is a malleable metal; sulphur, phosphorus and oxygen are non-metals.","e":"","src":["PGT 31 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7753","q":"Which of the following metals are not solid at room temperature?","o":["Iodine","Lithium","Mercury","Potassium"],"a":2,"x":"Mercury is the metal that is liquid at room temperature; iodine is a non-metal.","e":"","src":["PGT 22 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q8384","q":"According to Boyle’s law, which of the following statements is correct?","o":["At constant pressure, the force of a fixed amount of an ideal gas is inversely proportional to its volume.","At constant temperature, the pressure of a fixed amount of an ideal gas is inversely proportional to its volume.","At constant temperature, the pressure of a fixed amount of an ideal gas is directly proportional to its volume.","At constant pressure, the force of a fixed amount of a gas is directly proportional to its volume."],"a":1,"x":"Boyle's law: at constant temperature, the pressure of a fixed amount of gas is inversely proportional to its volume.","e":"","src":["PGT 5 Jul 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q8443","q":"Which of the following is an example of a fluid?","o":["Wood","Mercury","Marble","Glass"],"a":1,"x":"Mercury is a liquid, so it is a fluid; wood, marble and glass are solids.","e":"","src":["PGT 22 Jun 2023 S1"]},{"id":"q9595","q":"Which of the following scientists discovered the presence of Electrons in the year 1897?","o":["E. Goldstein","JJ Thomson","E. Rutherford","Neils Bohr"],"a":1,"x":"J.J. Thomson discovered the electron in 1897.","e":"","src":["PGT 28 Jun 2021"]},{"id":"q9596","q":"The atoms of different elements with different atomic numbers and that have the same mass number are called:","o":["Ions","Isobars","Isotopes","Neutrons"],"a":1,"x":"Isobars are atoms of different elements with the same mass number.","e":"","src":["PGT 28 Jun 2021"]},{"id":"q9645","q":"What number of elements are provided in the modern periodic table?","o":["94","108","118","100"],"a":2,"x":"The modern periodic table has 118 elements.","e":"","src":["PGT 29 Jun 2021"]},{"id":"q9870","q":"Whose 'Brownian-motion' paper provided the first real glimpse of the atomic nature of matter?","o":["Lev Landau","Jean Perrin","Paul Dirac","Albert Einstein"],"a":3,"x":"Einstein's 1905 paper on Brownian motion gave the first real evidence that atoms exist.","e":"","src":["PGT 25 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q9879","q":"Which s-block element, whose Latin name means sky blue, is derived from the colour of its flame?","o":["Lithium","Barium","Strontium","Cesium"],"a":3,"x":"Caesium comes from the Latin 'caesius', sky blue, after the blue lines in its spectrum.","e":"","src":["PGT 25 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q1632","q":"Who among the following proposed that structure of an atom is similar to that of Christmas pudding?","o":["J. Chadwick","Neil Bohr","Ernest Rutherford","J.J. Thomson"],"a":3,"x":"J.J. Thomson proposed the plum (Christmas) pudding model of the atom.","e":"","src":["Senior Scientific Assistant (Lie-Detection) 10 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q1672","q":"Which among the following metals is the lightest and least dense metal?","o":["Silver","Aluminium","Osmium","Lithium"],"a":3,"x":"Lithium is the lightest and least dense metal.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Computer) 10 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3332","q":"Which of the following elements has maximum atomic radius?","o":["Boron","Nitrogen","Lithium","Oxygen"],"a":2,"x":"Atomic radius decreases across a period, so lithium is the largest of these.","e":"","src":["Senior Scientific Assistant (Biology) 18 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3498","q":"In which year did J. Chadwick discover a sub-atomic particle which was eventually named neutron?","o":["1930","1932","1934","1936"],"a":1,"x":"James Chadwick discovered the neutron in 1932.","e":"","src":["Scientific Assistant (Lie-Detection) 1 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4116","q":"The Modern Periodic Law states −Properties of elements are a periodic function of their __________.","o":["atomic number","mass number","valency","mole"],"a":0,"x":"Modern periodic law: properties of elements are a periodic function of their atomic numbers.","e":"","src":["Veterinary and Livestock Inspector 23 Apr 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4443","q":"Which of the following metal is soft and can be cut with a knife?","o":["Zinc","Lead","Potassium","Nickel"],"a":2,"x":"Potassium is soft enough to cut with a knife.","e":"","src":["Laboratory Assistant (Documents) 24 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4721","q":"A lustrous Non-Metal is:","o":["Argon","Chlorine","Xenon","Iodine"],"a":3,"x":"Iodine is a lustrous non-metal.","e":"","src":["Laboratory Assistant (HRD Quality Control) 25 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4807","q":"What is the atomic number of Phosphorous?","o":["15","20","16","18"],"a":0,"x":"Phosphorus has atomic number 15.","e":"","src":["Scientific Assistant (Chemistry) 25 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4905","q":"Which of the following is also known as Deuterium?","o":["Heavy water","Oxygen","Nitrogen","Heavy hydrogen"],"a":3,"x":"Deuterium is heavy hydrogen, an isotope with one neutron.","e":"","src":["Assistant Bacteriologist 26 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5290","q":"____________ law states that equal volume of all gases under the same conditions of temperature and pressure contain equal number of molecules.","o":["Charles's","Bernoulli's","Boyles's","Avogadro’s"],"a":3,"x":"Avogadro's law: equal volumes of gases at the same temperature and pressure hold equal numbers of molecules.","e":"","src":["Assistant Engineer (Civil) 27 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5671","q":"In which state of matter electrons are ripped away from their nuclei, forming an electron “sea”?","o":["Solid","Liquid","Gas","Plasma"],"a":3,"x":"In plasma, electrons are stripped from their nuclei, forming an 'electron sea'; the Sun is made of plasma.","e":"","src":["Driver 30 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q6407","q":"The organic compounds in which R-O-R' functional group is present are called ____________.","o":["aldehydes","ketones","esters","ethers"],"a":3,"x":"Compounds with the R–O–R′ group are ethers.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Commercial Art) 14 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6428","q":"Which among the following is not a physical property of non-metals?","o":["They produce metallic lustre.","They are bad conductor of electricity.","They are bad conductor of heat.","They are electronegative."],"a":0,"x":"Non-metals do not have metallic lustre.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Digital Electronics) 7 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q8249","q":"Which of the following is a Noble Gas?","o":["Krypton","Hydrogen","Nitrogen","Fluorine"],"a":0,"x":"Krypton is a noble gas; hydrogen, nitrogen and fluorine are not.","e":"","src":["Librarian 10 Jul 2023 S1"]}]},{"id":"reactions","n":"Reactions, Acids and Bases","qs":[{"id":"q0309","q":"Which of the following is an example of a chemical change?","o":["Rusting of iron","Freezing water","Melting of ice","Boiling of water"],"a":0,"x":"Rusting of iron is a chemical change: iron reacts with oxygen and moisture to form a new substance, hydrated iron oxide. Freezing, melting and boiling are physical changes.","e":"Rusting can be prevented by galvanising (zinc coating), painting or oiling.","src":["ASO 20 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q1606","q":"pH value of neutral water should be-","o":["9","6","7","8"],"a":2,"x":"Pure (neutral) water has a pH of 7 at 25°C.","e":"Below 7 is acidic (lemon juice ~2); above 7 is basic (soap ~10).","src":["Patwari 17 Sep 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q0571","q":"Which among the following acids is also known as 'Muriatic acid'?","o":["Carbonic Acid","Sulphuric Acid","Hydrochloric Acid","Acetic Acid"],"a":2,"x":"Muriatic acid is the old commercial name for hydrochloric acid (HCl); our stomach makes HCl to digest food.","e":"Oil of vitriol: sulphuric acid. Aqua fortis: nitric acid.","src":["Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021","Junior Assistant 21 Aug 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1831","q":"Tooth decay starts when the pH of the mouth is lower than ______.","o":["7.2","6.8","5.5","7.8"],"a":2,"x":"Tooth enamel starts to dissolve when the mouth's pH falls below about 5.5, from acids made by bacteria breaking down sugars.","e":"Toothpaste is basic to neutralise the acid.","src":["Junior Clerk 11 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2151","q":"By using which of the following strong reducing agents can carboxylic acids be reduced to primary alcohols?","o":["Lithium Aluminium Hydroxide","Palladium","Sodium borohydride","Grignard Reagents"],"a":0,"x":"Lithium aluminium hydride (LiAlH₄) is a strong reducing agent that reduces carboxylic acids to primary alcohols; sodium borohydride is too weak for acids.","e":"LiAlH₄ = strong; NaBH₄ = mild.","src":["Junior Clerk 13 Nov 2021 S3"],"k":"Option A is misprinted as 'Lithium Aluminium Hydroxide'; the reagent meant is lithium aluminium hydride (LiAlH₄)."},{"id":"q2669","q":"Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct? I. Acid turns blue litmus red. II. Base turns red litmus blue.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"An acid turns blue litmus red and a base turns red litmus blue, so both are correct.","e":"Acid → red; base → blue.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6109","q":"Which of the following pairs of acid/base – found in, is NOT correct? I. Ascorbic acid – Ant’s sting II. Magnesium hydroxide – Soap III. Oxalic acid – Spinach IV. Calcium hydroxide – Window cleaner","o":["I, II and IV","I, II and III","II and III","III and IV"],"a":0,"x":"Oxalic acid in spinach is correct. Ant sting has formic acid, magnesium hydroxide is an antacid, and window cleaners use ammonium hydroxide, so I, II and IV are wrong.","e":"","src":["Junior Clerk 9 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6908","q":"The ability of carbon to form bonds with other atoms of carbon is termed ___________.","o":["substitution","combustion","oxidation","catenation"],"a":3,"x":"Catenation is carbon's ability to form bonds with other carbon atoms, making long chains and rings.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 14 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q7001","q":"Which of the following is not an example of a strong acid?","o":["Hydrochloric acid","Sulphuric acid","Carbonic acid","Nitric acid"],"a":2,"x":"Carbonic acid is a weak acid; hydrochloric, sulphuric and nitric acids are strong.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 14 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7003","q":"When an acid and a base react together to form salt and water, it is called ____________.","o":["Neutralisation Reaction","Decomposition Reaction","Displacement Reaction","Combination Reaction"],"a":0,"x":"An acid reacting with a base to give salt and water is a neutralisation reaction.","e":"","src":["Stenographer 14 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q10125","q":"When the litmus solution is neither acidic nor basic, its colour is ______.","o":["Blue","Red","Purple","Green"],"a":2,"x":"Litmus solution is purple when it is neither acidic nor basic.","e":"","src":["Assistant Superintendent 20 Jul 2024 S3"]},{"id":"q8918","q":"Why do chips manufacturers flush the packets of chips with nitrogen gas?","o":["To prevent oxidation and rancidity of oils in chips","To add flavour to the chips","To keep the chips warm","To increase the weight of the packet"],"a":0,"x":"Chips packets are filled with nitrogen to stop oxidation, which makes the oils rancid.","e":"","src":["MTS 3 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q8956","q":"Why does a cut apple turn brown when exposed to air?","o":["It reacts with carbon dioxide","It dries up due to evaporation","It reacts with oxygen","It absorbs moisture from the air"],"a":2,"x":"A cut apple turns brown because its enzymes react with oxygen in the air (oxidation).","e":"","src":["MTS 3 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q9278","q":"Formic acid is naturally found in ______________.","o":["Grapes","Milk","Curd","Ant’s sting"],"a":3,"x":"Formic (methanoic) acid is found in ant stings.","e":"","src":["MTS 8 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q3457","q":"Which of the following pair correct? I. Rusting – Physical change II. Cutting of paper – Chemical change","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":3,"x":"Rusting is a chemical change (new substance, iron oxide) and cutting paper a physical change, so both pairs are wrong.","e":"","src":["Store Keeper 19 Apr 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3674","q":"When a copper vessel is exposed to moist air for long, it acquires a dull coating. What is the colour of that coating?","o":["Red","White","Green","Brown"],"a":2,"x":"Copper exposed to moist air gets a green coating of basic copper carbonate.","e":"Silver tarnishes black; iron rusts brown.","src":["Fire Operator 20 Apr 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2755","q":"Which among the following acids is not an example of a weak acid?","o":["Acetic Acid","Formic Acid","Carbonic Acid","Nitric Acid"],"a":3,"x":"Nitric acid is a strong acid; acetic, formic and carbonic acids are weak acids.","e":"Strong acids: HCl, H₂SO₄, HNO₃.","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2826","q":"Which among the following acids is known as 'oil of vitriol'?","o":["Hydrochloric Acid","Sulphuric Acid","Nitric Acid","Chloric Acid"],"a":1,"x":"Sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄) is called 'oil of vitriol'; it is also known as the 'king of chemicals'.","e":"","src":["Ahlmad 16 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0926","q":"Who first reported the hydroboration–oxidation reaction in the late 1950s?","o":["Stephanie Louise Kwolek","Hermann Emil Louis Fischer","Herbert Charles Brown","George Charles de Hevesy"],"a":2,"x":"Herbert Charles Brown first reported the hydroboration–oxidation reaction in the late 1950s; he shared the 1979 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for boron chemistry.","e":"Hydroboration gives anti-Markovnikov alcohols from alkenes.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q0997","q":"Which natural substance is used to produce litmus?","o":["Algae","Bacteria","Fungi","Lichens"],"a":3,"x":"Litmus is extracted from lichens; it turns red in acids and blue in bases.","e":"Acid turns blue litmus red.","src":["PRT 19 Feb 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1152","q":"turmeric gives red color when it reacts with _____________","o":["Base","Salt","Water","Acid"],"a":0,"x":"Turmeric is a natural indicator: it turns reddish-brown with a base, such as soap solution, and stays yellow in acid.","e":"Other natural indicators: litmus (from lichens), red cabbage, China rose.","src":["PRT 26 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q1231","q":"The pH of a 0.01 N acid solution that is 80% ionised will be ______.","o":["0.0269","0.2096","20.09","2.0969"],"a":3,"x":"For a 0.01 N acid that is 80% ionised, [H⁺] = 0.01 × 0.8 = 0.008 M, so pH = −log(8 × 10⁻³) = 3 − 0.903 ≈ 2.097.","e":"pH = −log[H⁺]; a lower pH means a stronger acid.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1233","q":"According to Le Chatelier's principle, which conditions mainly shift the equilibrium of the Haber process towards ammonia formation?","o":["Low temperature and low pressure","Low pressure and high temperature with a catalyst","High pressure and low temperature","Low pressure and high temperature"],"a":2,"x":"N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ is exothermic and reduces the number of gas molecules, so high pressure and low temperature favour ammonia (Le Chatelier). In practice about 450°C, 200 atm and an iron catalyst are used.","e":"A catalyst speeds up the reaction but does not shift the equilibrium.","src":["PRT 31 Mar 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q4230","q":"Which of the following is not a redox (oxidation-reduction) reaction?","o":["Rusting","Hydration","Combustion","Lightning"],"a":1,"x":"Hydration is not a redox reaction; rusting and combustion are.","e":"","src":["Nursery Teacher 23 Oct 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q1510","q":"The letter 'p' in pH stands for ‘potenz’ which in German means __________.","o":["power","proton","potential","percent"],"a":0,"x":"In pH, the 'p' comes from the German 'potenz', meaning power (of hydrogen-ion concentration): pH = −log[H⁺].","e":"pH 7 is neutral; below 7 acidic; above 7 basic.","src":["TGT 24 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q1655","q":"Which of the following statement is true? I. Bases are bitter in taste and soapy to touch. II. Acids are sour in taste","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Bases are bitter and soapy to touch; acids are sour. Both statements are correct.","e":"","src":["TGT 10 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2349","q":"Which pair of natural source – acid is correct? I. Curd – Lactic acid II. Tomato – Oxalic acid","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Curd contains lactic acid and tomato contains oxalic acid (NCERT Class 10), so both pairs are correct.","e":"","src":["TGT 14 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3540","q":"Burning of coal is an example of which reaction?","o":["Decomposition reaction","Displacement reaction","Double displacement reaction","Combination reaction"],"a":3,"x":"Burning coal (C + O₂ → CO₂) is a combination reaction.","e":"","src":["TGT 1 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q4747","q":"Which of the following is a natural chemical indicator of an acid or base?","o":["Litmus","Methyl orange","Phenolphthalein","Diphenylcarbazone"],"a":0,"x":"Litmus, made from lichens, is a natural acid–base indicator; methyl orange and phenolphthalein are synthetic.","e":"","src":["TGT 25 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5862","q":"Which of the following statement is correct? I. Respiration is an exothermic reaction. II. Decomposition of vegetable matter into compost is an exothermic reaction.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Respiration and the decomposition of vegetable matter into compost are both exothermic.","e":"","src":["TGT 6 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6239","q":"Which of the following is correct sequence of reactivity of metals?","o":["Zn > Pb > Cu > Ag","Zn > Pb > Ag > Cu","Zn > Cu > Pb > Ag","Cu > Zn > Pb > Ag"],"a":0,"x":"In the reactivity series, zinc > lead > copper > silver.","e":"","src":["TGT 9 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q6305","q":"Which of the following statement is correct? I. 5-8 percent solution of acetic acid in water is called vinegar. II. Ethane has 6 covalent bonds.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"x":"Vinegar is a 5–8% solution of acetic acid; ethane has 7 covalent bonds, not 6, so only I is correct.","e":"","src":["TGT 9 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6451","q":"Tamarind is the natural source of which of the following acids?","o":["Tartaric acid","Acetic acid","Oxalic acid","Oxalic acid & Acetic acid"],"a":0,"x":"Tamarind is a natural source of tartaric acid.","e":"","src":["TGT 26 Jul 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q0864","q":"What is an aqueous solution containing a mixture of a weak acid and its salt, or a weak base and its salt, called?","o":["Hypotonic solution","Unsaturated solution","Buffer solution","Saturated solution"],"a":2,"x":"A buffer solution contains a weak acid and its salt (or a weak base and its salt) and resists changes in pH when a little acid or base is added.","e":"Blood is buffered at pH about 7.4 by the carbonic acid–bicarbonate system.","src":["PGT 24 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q7774","q":"The process of conversion of sugar into alcohol is known as _________.","o":["Fermentation","Esterification","Hydrogenation","Hydroxylation"],"a":0,"x":"Converting sugar into alcohol is fermentation.","e":"","src":["PGT 22 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7796","q":"What does the P in pH Value stands for?","o":["Purity","Physical","Potenz","Phosphorus"],"a":2,"x":"The 'p' in pH comes from the German 'Potenz' (power), meaning the power of hydrogen ions.","e":"","src":["PGT 23 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q9626","q":"What temperature is known as the lowest temperature at which a substance can catch fire?","o":["Ignition Temperature","Fire Temperature","Oxidization Temperature","Flaming Temperature"],"a":0,"x":"Ignition temperature is the lowest temperature at which a substance catches fire.","e":"","src":["PGT 29 Jun 2021"]},{"id":"q2253","q":"Which of the following statement is CORRECT? I. Potassium and sodium react violently with cold water. II. Aqua regia is a highly corrosive fuming liquid.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"Potassium and sodium react violently with cold water, and aqua regia is a highly corrosive fuming liquid.","e":"","src":["Senior Scientific Assistant (Ballistics) 13 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3081","q":"Which is a natural olfactory indicator?","o":["Potato","Cauliflower","Onion","Brinjal"],"a":2,"x":"Onion is a natural olfactory indicator, since its smell disappears in a base.","e":"","src":["Draftsman 17 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5346","q":"The process due to which fatty food becomes rancid is called:","o":["Oxidation","Reduction","Dehydration","Hydration"],"a":0,"x":"Fatty food turns rancid through oxidation.","e":"","src":["Laboratory Assistant (Physics) 28 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q6706","q":"Which of the following statement is correct? I. Non metallic oxides are basic in nature. II. Metallic oxides are acidic in nature.","o":["Only I","Only II","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":3,"x":"Both are wrong: non-metallic oxides are acidic and metallic oxides are basic.","e":"","src":["Investigator 26 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7376","q":"The process of depositing a layer of zinc on iron is called ______.","o":["rusting","crystallisation","galvanisation","deposition"],"a":2,"x":"Coating iron with a layer of zinc to prevent rusting is galvanisation.","e":"","src":["Physical Education Teacher 2 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7399","q":"Which base is found in lime water?","o":["Ammonium Hydroxide","Calcium Hydroxide","Sodium Hydroxide","Potassium Hydroxide"],"a":1,"x":"Lime water is a solution of calcium hydroxide.","e":"","src":["Physical Education Teacher 2 Aug 2021"]}]},{"id":"everyday","n":"Chemistry in Everyday Life","qs":[{"id":"q0036","q":"Select the correct statement with reference to reducing sugars.","o":["All disaccharides are reducing sugars.","Reducing sugars do not react with Tollen's or Fehling's reagent.","Sucrose is a common example of a reducing sugar.","Reducing sugars can reduce mild oxidising agents like Fehling’s solution."],"a":3,"x":"Reducing sugars have a free aldehyde or ketone group, so they reduce mild oxidising agents such as Fehling's and Tollens' reagents. Glucose is one; sucrose is not.","e":"Glucose turns blue Fehling's solution into a brick-red precipitate.","src":["ASO 18 Aug 2025 S1"]},{"id":"q0389","q":"Select the odd one with reference to carbohydrates.","o":["C6H12O6","C12H22O11","C6H12O5","CH3COOH"],"a":3,"x":"C₆H₁₂O₆ (glucose), C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ (sucrose) and C₆H₁₂O₅ (a deoxy sugar) are carbohydrates. CH₃COOH is acetic acid, a carboxylic acid, not a carbohydrate.","e":"Vinegar is about 5–8% acetic acid.","src":["ASO 21 Aug 2025 S3"]},{"id":"q1943","q":"Which acid is found in spinach?","o":["Oxalic","Tartaric","Formic","Lactic"],"a":0,"x":"Spinach contains oxalic acid. Tamarind has tartaric acid, ant stings formic acid, curd lactic acid.","e":"Spinach → oxalic; tomato → oxalic too.","src":["Junior Clerk 12 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2441","q":"What is the IUPAC name of Vinegar?","o":["Ethanol","Sodium Chloride","Sodium Bicarbonate","Acetic Acid"],"a":3,"x":"Vinegar is a 5–8% solution of acetic acid in water.","e":"Tamarind: tartaric acid; lemon: citric acid.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S2"],"k":"Strictly, vinegar has no IUPAC name of its own; the IUPAC name of acetic acid is ethanoic acid. DSSSB's key means acetic acid."},{"id":"q2487","q":"Which of the following chemicals are present in heads of safety matches?","o":["Red phosphorus and white phosphorus","Antimony trisulphide and potassium chlorate","Potassium chloride and phosphorus trisulphate","Antimony chlorate and red phosphorus"],"a":1,"x":"Safety match heads contain antimony trisulphide and potassium chlorate; the striking surface has powdered glass and red phosphorus.","e":"Red phosphorus is on the box, not the stick.","src":["Junior Clerk 15 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q2525","q":"In which of the following medicines is acetylsalicyclic acid used?","o":["Antacid","Aspirin","Inhalers","Anti-diabetic medicines"],"a":1,"x":"Aspirin is acetylsalicylic acid, a painkiller that reduces fever and inflammation.","e":"Aspirin also thins the blood.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q2667","q":"Which acid is found in tomatos?","o":["Acetic","Oxalic","Lactic","Methanoic"],"a":1,"x":"Tomatoes contain oxalic acid (along with citric and malic acids).","e":"Tomato and spinach: oxalic acid.","src":["Junior Clerk 16 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q1294","q":"Which of the following is present in chalk?","o":["Sodium Hydroxide","Potassium Nitrate","Sodium Chloride","Calcium Carbonate"],"a":3,"x":"Chalk is calcium carbonate (CaCO₃); limestone and marble are also CaCO₃.","e":"Heating CaCO₃ gives quicklime (CaO) and CO₂.","src":["Stenographer 19 Apr 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q10126","q":"Which of the following is used in the manufacture of borax?","o":["Washing Soda","Calcium Phosphate","Baking Soda","Chlorine"],"a":0,"x":"Washing soda (sodium carbonate) is used to manufacture borax (NCERT Class 10).","e":"","src":["Assistant Superintendent 20 Jul 2024 S3"]},{"id":"q9481","q":"What is generally used in the kitchen for making crispy pakoras?","o":["Bleaching powder","Milk of Magnesia","Washing Soda","Baking Soda"],"a":3,"x":"Baking soda releases carbon dioxide when heated, making pakoras crisp and fluffy.","e":"","src":["Assistant Superintendent 14 Jul 2024 S1"]},{"id":"q4493","q":"Which of the following is not a type of sugar?","o":["Fructose","Galactose","Lactose","Whey"],"a":3,"x":"Fructose, galactose and lactose are sugars; whey is the watery part of milk left after curdling.","e":"","src":["Office Superintendent 24 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q8920","q":"What is the function of emulsifiers in food products?","o":["Preventing spoilage","Stabilising oil and water mixtures","Increasing shelf life","Enhancing colour"],"a":1,"x":"Emulsifiers stabilise mixtures of oil and water so they do not separate.","e":"","src":["MTS 3 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q8958","q":"What is the primary pigment found in black tea?","o":["Thearubigins","Betalains","Chlorophyll","Melanin"],"a":0,"x":"Thearubigins are the main pigments that give black tea its colour.","e":"","src":["MTS 3 Mar 2026 S2"]},{"id":"q9202","q":"Which among the following acids causes the sour taste in cold drinks like Coke, Pepsi, and Campa?","o":["Lactic acid","Hydrochloric acid","Sulfuric acid","Phosphoric acid"],"a":3,"x":"Phosphoric acid gives cola drinks their sour taste.","e":"","src":["MTS 8 Mar 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q9315","q":"Which compound do shaving soaps contain to prevent rapid drying?","o":["Sodium hydroxide","Glycerol","Potassium hydroxide","Sodium benzoate"],"a":1,"x":"Shaving soaps contain glycerol to keep them from drying quickly.","e":"","src":["MTS 24 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q3419","q":"Which of the following acid is contained by curd?","o":["Acetic acid","Lactic acid","Oxalic acid","Tartaric acid"],"a":1,"x":"Curd contains lactic acid, made by Lactobacillus bacteria.","e":"","src":["Store Keeper 19 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3866","q":"Which among the following is known as laughing gas?","o":["Calcium oxide","Sulphuric acid","Nitrous oxide","Ferric oxide"],"a":2,"x":"Nitrous oxide (N₂O) is called laughing gas; it is used as a mild anaesthetic.","e":"","src":["Store Keeper 22 Apr 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3632","q":"Which of the following is a naturally occuring polymer?","o":["Nylon","Rayon","Terylene","Cotton"],"a":3,"x":"Cotton (cellulose) is a natural polymer; nylon and terylene are synthetic and rayon is semi-synthetic.","e":"","src":["Fire Operator 20 Apr 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q2975","q":"Which of the following chemicals is present in bleaches used in household laundering and sanitizing?","o":["Potassium permanganate","Sodium hypochlorite","Sodium hydroxide","Calcium carbonate"],"a":1,"x":"Household bleach contains sodium hypochlorite (NaOCl); bleaching powder is calcium oxychloride.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 17 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3209","q":"Which of the following elements is oxidised in the process of anodising?","o":["Zinc","Aluminium","Lead","Mercury"],"a":1,"x":"Anodising thickens the protective oxide layer on aluminium by making it the anode in electrolysis.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 18 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3913","q":"Whose chemical name is Sodium bicarbonate?","o":["Baking Soda","Phenyl","Castic soda","Wax"],"a":0,"x":"Baking soda is sodium hydrogen carbonate (sodium bicarbonate), NaHCO₃; caustic soda is sodium hydroxide.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 22 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3914","q":"Which gas is responsible for irritation in eyes while chopping an onion?","o":["Methane","Nitrogen","Sulfur","Helium"],"a":2,"x":"Cutting an onion releases a sulphur compound (syn-propanethial-S-oxide) that irritates the eyes.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 22 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4009","q":"___________ is applied to inanimate objects.","o":["Tranquilizers","Disinfectants","Analgesics","Antibiotics"],"a":1,"x":"Disinfectants are applied to non-living objects such as floors and instruments; antiseptics are used on living tissue.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 22 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q4289","q":"Which of the following is not a fermented preparation of rice and black gram?","o":["Dosa","Uthappam","Idli","Khichdi"],"a":3,"x":"Dosa, idli and uthappam batters are fermented from rice and black gram; khichdi is not fermented.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 24 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q4619","q":"Which of the following carbon compounds is one of the main ingredients in a fire extinguisher?","o":["carbon monoxide","carbon dioxide","carbon disulfide","carbon halides/ carboranes"],"a":1,"x":"Carbon dioxide is used in fire extinguishers because it is heavier than air, does not burn and cuts off oxygen.","e":"","src":["Fee Collector Sub Inspector Auction Recorder 25 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5488","q":"Fully hydrogenated fatty acids are called ______.","o":["Unsaturated fat","Saturated fat","Drenched fat","Inundated fat"],"a":1,"x":"Fully hydrogenated fats are saturated fats.","e":"","src":["Accountant 29 Nov 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q0968","q":"What is the main acid component in vinegar?","o":["Citric acid","Acetic acid","Tannic acid","Nitric acid"],"a":1,"x":"Vinegar is a dilute solution (about 5–8%) of acetic (ethanoic) acid, CH₃COOH.","e":"Lemon has citric acid; tea has tannic acid; curd has lactic acid.","src":["PRT 18 Feb 2026 S3"]},{"id":"q1258","q":"Pyrolusite ore (MnO2) is used for the preparation of _____.","o":["sodium chromate","potassium dichromate","potassium permanganate","sodium manganate"],"a":2,"x":"Pyrolusite (MnO₂) is the main manganese ore and is used to make potassium permanganate (KMnO₄).","e":"Potassium dichromate comes from chromite ore.","src":["PRT 10 Apr 2026 S1"]},{"id":"q1480","q":"Which of the following acids is found in orange?","o":["Citric acid","Oxalic acid","Methanoic acid","Lactic acid"],"a":0,"x":"Oranges and lemons contain citric acid.","e":"Tomato: oxalic acid. Ant sting: methanoic (formic) acid. Curd: lactic acid.","src":["TGT 7 Feb 2024 S2"]},{"id":"q3377","q":"The active ingredient in Aqua tabs (self dissolving tablets that purify water) are:","o":["Sodium Bismuthane (NaBiO3)","Sodium Octamolybdate (Na2MO8O25.4h2O)","Sodium Uranate (Na2UO4)","Sodium Dichloroisocyanurate (NaDCC)"],"a":3,"x":"Aquatabs water-purifying tablets contain sodium dichloroisocyanurate (NaDCC), which releases chlorine.","e":"","src":["TGT 18 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q3378","q":"Which among the following chemical compounds is not used as a fertilizer or in the production of fertilizers?","o":["Fluorine","Magnesium","Potassium","Nitrogen"],"a":0,"x":"Nitrogen, potassium and magnesium are plant nutrients used in fertilisers; fluorine is not.","e":"","src":["TGT 18 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5603","q":"The sodium stearate molecule has:","o":["a hydrophobic head and a hydrophilic tail","a hydrophilic head and a hydrophilic tail","a hydrophilic head and a hydrophobic tail","a hydrophobic head and a hydrophobic tail"],"a":2,"x":"A soap molecule such as sodium stearate has a hydrophilic (water-loving) head and a hydrophobic (water-repelling) tail.","e":"","src":["TGT 30 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5709","q":"Which of the following acids is widely used in rural India to prevent snake infestation?","o":["Glycolic acid","Gluconic acid","Carbolic acid","Acetic acid"],"a":2,"x":"Carbolic acid (phenol) is used in rural India to keep snakes away.","e":"","src":["TGT 4 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q5838","q":"What is the basic method of water purification that is used in our everyday kitchen electronic water purifiers?","o":["Desalination","Iodine Addition","Reverse Osmosis","Distillation"],"a":2,"x":"Most household electric water purifiers use reverse osmosis (RO).","e":"","src":["TGT 5 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5899","q":"What is the common name of compound CaOCl2?","o":["Washing soda","Plaster of Paris","Bleaching powder","Quick lime"],"a":2,"x":"CaOCl₂ is bleaching powder.","e":"","src":["TGT 6 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q6450","q":"What material is used for coating Non-stick cooking utensils?","o":["Teflon","PVC","Black Paint","polystyrene"],"a":0,"x":"Non-stick cookware is coated with Teflon (PTFE).","e":"","src":["TGT 26 Jul 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q7486","q":"Which base is found in window cleaner?","o":["Calcium Hydroxide","Potassium Hydroxide","Sodium Hydroxide","Ammonium Hydroxide"],"a":3,"x":"Window cleaners contain ammonium hydroxide.","e":"","src":["TGT 1 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q7983","q":"Which of the following statements is correct regarding natural rubber?","o":["Caprolactam is monomer of natural rubber.","Isoprene is monomer of natural rubber.","Bakelite is monomer of natural rubber.","Styrene is monomer of natural rubber."],"a":1,"x":"Natural rubber is a polymer of isoprene.","e":"","src":["TGT 6 Jul 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q7984","q":"Which of the following drugs is used as a histamine blocker?","o":["Iproniazid","Phenelzine","Ranitidine","Aspirin"],"a":2,"x":"Ranitidine is a histamine (H2) blocker that reduces stomach acid.","e":"","src":["TGT 6 Jul 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q8013","q":"Which of the following standards is used to certify the purity of gold in India?","o":["ISI","Hallmark","FPO","Agmark"],"a":1,"x":"Hallmarking certifies the purity of gold in India (BIS hallmark).","e":"","src":["TGT 6 Jul 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q9810","q":"Which of the following substances is NOT used as a preservative?","o":["Sodium Bicarbonate","Sugar","Salt","Potassium Meta Bisulphite (KMS)"],"a":0,"x":"Sodium bicarbonate (baking soda) is not a preservative; sugar, salt and potassium metabisulphite are.","e":"","src":["TGT 17 Aug 2025"]},{"id":"q9842","q":"What is the full form of ‘RO’ in ‘RO Water Purifying System’?","o":["Reverse Orientation","Reserve Osmosis","Recover Osmosis","Reverse Osmosis"],"a":3,"x":"RO stands for Reverse Osmosis.","e":"","src":["Special Educator 14 Jul 2024 S1"]},{"id":"q0839","q":"Which of the following acidic functional groups is present in picric acid?","o":["hydroxyl (-OH)","carboxylic acid (-COOH)","phosphoric acid (-PO4H2)","sulfonic acid (-SO3H) Q The Gandamak treaty was signed in 1879 between which of the following parties?"],"a":0,"x":"Picric acid (2,4,6-trinitrophenol) is a phenol: its acidic group is the hydroxyl (–OH), made strongly acidic by three nitro groups.","e":"It was used as an explosive and as a yellow dye.","src":["PGT 26 Sep 2025"]},{"id":"q7644","q":"Which of the following elements is used for making Electric Bulb Filament?","o":["Copper","Aluminium","Lead","Tungsten"],"a":3,"x":"Electric bulb filaments are made of tungsten, which has a very high melting point.","e":"","src":["PGT 29 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7645","q":"Which of the following chemical compound is also known as Washing Soda?","o":["Sodium Carbonate","Calcium Carbonate","Calcium Bicarbonate","Sodium Bicarbonate"],"a":0,"x":"Washing soda is sodium carbonate (Na₂CO₃·10H₂O).","e":"","src":["PGT 29 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q7795","q":"Which of the following is used in making Pencils?","o":["Graphite","Silicon","Charcoal","Phosphorus"],"a":0,"x":"Pencil 'lead' is made of graphite mixed with clay.","e":"","src":["PGT 23 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q8469","q":"Naphthalene balls are a product of which of the following substances?","o":["Coke","Petroleum","Coal tar","Diesel"],"a":2,"x":"Naphthalene balls are obtained from coal tar.","e":"","src":["PGT 22 Jun 2023 S2"]},{"id":"q8556","q":"Which of the following ions are responsible for hardness of water?","o":["Ca2+ and Mg2+","Na+ and Mg2+","Ca2+ and Na+","Ba2+ and Mg2+"],"a":0,"x":"Calcium (Ca²⁺) and magnesium (Mg²⁺) ions cause hardness of water.","e":"","src":["PGT 3 Nov 2022"]},{"id":"q9520","q":"Bell Metal is an alloy which is made up of ____________.","o":["copper and aluminium","copper and zinc","copper and nickel","copper and tin"],"a":3,"x":"Bell metal is an alloy of copper and tin.","e":"","src":["PGT 30 Jun 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q9540","q":"____________ is also known as quick lime.","o":["Calcium sulphate","Calcium oxide","Potassium carbonate","Potassium hydroxide"],"a":1,"x":"Quick lime is calcium oxide (CaO).","e":"","src":["PGT 30 Jun 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q9855","q":"The brine solution represents _____.","o":["KCl in ethanol","KCl in water","NaCl in ethanol","NaCl in water"],"a":3,"x":"Brine is a strong solution of sodium chloride in water.","e":"","src":["PGT 17 Jul 2025"]},{"id":"q10005","q":"Which of the following fruits oxalic acid is present?","o":["Orange","Banana","Apple","Tomato"],"a":3,"x":"Tomatoes contain oxalic acid.","e":"","src":["Junior Engineer (Electrical) or Section Officer (Electrical) 22 Jun 2022 S2"]},{"id":"q2798","q":"Which of the following gases is filled in bags of chips to prevent the chips from getting oxidised?","o":["Nitrogen","Oxygen","Hydrogen","Hydrogen sulphide"],"a":0,"x":"Chip packets are filled with nitrogen to stop oxidation.","e":"","src":["Inspecting Officer 16 Oct 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3176","q":"Milk of magnesia is also known as:","o":["magnesium carbonate","magnesium chloride","magnesium hydroxide","magnesium sulphate"],"a":2,"x":"Milk of magnesia is magnesium hydroxide.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Plastic) 18 Nov 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q3288","q":"Which of the following substance is used in soda-acid fire extinguishers?","o":["Washing soda","Baking soda","Bleaching powder","Plaster of Paris"],"a":1,"x":"Soda-acid fire extinguishers use baking soda (sodium bicarbonate).","e":"","src":["Senior Scientific Assistant (Document) 18 Sep 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q3728","q":"Detergents are ammonium or sulphonate salts of long chain:","o":["hydrofluoric acids","carboxylic acids","hydrochloric acids","nitric acids"],"a":1,"x":"Detergents are ammonium or sulphonate salts of long-chain carboxylic acids (NCERT).","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Modern Office Practice) English 21 Oct 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q4254","q":"Which of the following types of alcohol is present in the beverages such as vodka, beer and wine?","o":["Ethyl alcohol","Isopropyl alcohol","Propyl alcohol","Methyl alcohol"],"a":0,"x":"Alcoholic drinks contain ethyl alcohol (ethanol).","e":"","src":["Workshop Instructor 24 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q4669","q":"Table salt is also known as:","o":["iodine","sodium chloride","helium","potassium chloride"],"a":1,"x":"Table salt is sodium chloride (NaCl).","e":"","src":["Scientific Assistant (biology) 25 Nov 2021 S1"]},{"id":"q5268","q":"Main constituent of biogas is ______.","o":["Butane","Methane","Ethane","Propane"],"a":1,"x":"Methane is the main part of biogas.","e":"","src":["Senior Scientific Assistant (Physics) 27 Sep 2021 S3"]},{"id":"q5345","q":"What is the scientific name for table sugar?","o":["Carbohydrates","Fructose","Glucose","Sucrose"],"a":3,"x":"Table sugar is sucrose.","e":"","src":["Laboratory Assistant (Physics) 28 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q5347","q":"What is the name and chemical formula of laughing gas?","o":["Nitrogen dioxide (NO2)","Nitrous oxide (N2O)","Nitric oxide (NO)","Nitrate (NO3)"],"a":1,"x":"Laughing gas is nitrous oxide (N₂O).","e":"","src":["Laboratory Assistant (Physics) 28 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q5795","q":"Electrum is the alloy of gold and ___________.","o":["zinc","silver","aluminium","iron"],"a":1,"x":"Electrum is a natural alloy of gold and silver.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Electrical) 5 Sep 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6327","q":"Invar is an alloy that is made up of _______________.","o":["nickel and gold","iron and gold","iron and silver","iron and nickel"],"a":3,"x":"Invar is an alloy of iron and nickel.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Beauty Culture) 26 Jul 2021"]},{"id":"q6406","q":"Solder is an alloy that is made up of lead and ________.","o":["tin","copper","aluminium","silver"],"a":0,"x":"Solder is an alloy of lead and tin.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Commercial Art) 14 Aug 2021 S2"]},{"id":"q6532","q":"____________ acid is also known as Methanoic acid.","o":["Erucic acid","Acetic acid","Lactic acid","Formic acid"],"a":3,"x":"Formic acid is methanoic acid.","e":"","src":["Technical Assistant (Garments Fabrication Technology) 2 Aug 2021"]},{"id":"q8660","q":"Nichrome wire is an alloy of:","o":["Nickel and Aluminium","Nickel and Copper","Nickel and Chromium","Nickel and iron"],"a":2,"x":"Nichrome is an alloy of nickel and chromium.","e":"","src":["Shift Incharge 25 Jun 2023 S2"]}]}]});
